<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Birkhan, H. (1997) Die Kelten. Vienna.</li><li>Drinkwater, J. F. (2014) [1983] Roman Gaul: The Three Provinces, 58 BC–AD 260. Abingdon: Routledge.</li><li>Kendrick, T. D. (1927) The Druids.</li><li>Piggott, S. (1968) The Druids.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: implied by the estimate that the one million people who died in Caesar's Gallic Wars were probably one Gaul in twelve.</p></section>
